mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mover><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi>i</mi><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"OP\">∑</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi data-mjx-texclass=\"OP\" mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><msubsup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mover><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mtext>ML</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> The bias does not disappear as <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"m\\to\\infty\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45A TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2192\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c221E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>. By the law of large numbers,\n<span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\hat{\\sigma}^2_{\\text{ML}}\\to\\sigma^2/2\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msubsup><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mover><mjx-over style=\"padding-bottom: 0.105em; padding-left: 0.285em; margin-bottom: -0.531em;\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" style=\"width: 0; margin-left: -0.25em;\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c5E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-over><mjx-base><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D70E TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-base></mjx-mover></mjx-TeXAtom><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: -0.3em; margin-left: 0;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-spacer style=\"margin-top: 0.18em;\"></mjx-spacer><mjx-TeXAtom size=\"s\" texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c4D\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c4C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext></mjx-TeXAtom></mjx-script></mjx-msubsup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2192\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-msup space=\"4\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D70E TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2F\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-TeXAtom><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mover><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mtext>ML</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mo>/</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>\nwith probability one, so the MLE is <em>inconsistent</em>. The unbiased\n<span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"s^2=\\text{SSE}/(n-r)\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D460 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c53\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c53\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c45\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2F\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-TeXAtom><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45B TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45F TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext>SSE</mtext><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mo>/</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> is exactly\ntwice as large and is consistent. This is the example of Neyman and\nScott (1948). It shows that the good large-sample behaviour of maximum\nlikelihood can fail when the number of nuisance parameters grows with\nthe data. Restricted maximum likelihood, which removes the mean before\nestimating the variance (<span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"exr-opt-reml\">@exr-opt-reml</span>), repairs it, and <a href=\"../ch32-linear-mixed-models/index.html\">Chapter 32</a> builds\nvariance-component estimation on that idea (<span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"def-mix-reml\">@def-mix-reml</span>).</p>","file":"ch07-optimality/03-maximum-likelihood.html"}